{
  "abstract": "Can an activation reader keep two variables apart when both queries receive exactly the same physical states? We test complete first/latest literal-value reports with a fixed token decoder, local expression grammar, activation identifier classifier and positional join. Earlier single-query evidence did not establish an incremental benefit of the classifier. After retaining a failed source-construction pilot, a coupled development construction motivates new instance-disjoint Qwen3-8B sources and a prospectively committed Qwen3-1.7B transfer on the same prompts. Each model retains 96 physical prefixes from 48 problems; 76 and 80 histories meet the fixed joint eligibility gate. Against strict parsing of the same reconstructed tokens, the fixed nine-fit mean gains 49.49 percentage points of supported endpoints [43.29, 55.48] on 8B and 29.86 points [21.73, 37.76] on 1.7B, while contradiction differences are -16.37 [-23.16, -10.22] and -7.78 [-12.86, -2.94]. Both models also pass the fixed comparison with query-blind public attribution and matched-versus-donor controls. Complete four-endpoint recovery reaches 63.0% and 48.6%, below strict source text at 80.3% and 76.3%. Paired uncertainty preserves 24 two-problem donor blocks; all fitted seeds, rejected pilots, excluded histories and author-only native audits are retained. Public saved-prediction replays verify both complete analyses. The result supports an operational benefit of identifier-sensitive composition in explicitly elicited trial histories within two developed families. It does not establish unseen-family generalization, a native causal binding mechanism, hidden beliefs or matched-access superiority to Activation Oracles.",
